const LESSON2_TOOLS = {"flashcards": [["Why preconception preparation?", "Early development begins before pregnancy may be recognized. Nutrition and clinical preparation support health but cannot guarantee conception or prevent every complication."], ["Pituitary versus ovarian hormones", "FSH and LH are pituitary hormones; estrogen and progesterone are ovarian hormones. In the simplified 28-day model, the LH surge occurs around day 14—not a universal personal schedule."], ["Follicular versus luteal phase", "The follicular phase begins with menstruation and ends at ovulation. The luteal phase follows ovulation; the corpus luteum produces progesterone."], ["Prostaglandins and PMDD", "Prostaglandins contribute to uterine contractions and cramps. PMDD involves clinically significant cyclical emotional/physical symptoms; irritability alone is not a diagnosis."], ["Energy availability", "Energy remaining after exercise expenditure for other physiological functions. Nutrient-dense food does not guarantee enough energy; body size does not diagnose under-fuelling."], ["Adipose tissue and insulin", "Adipose tissue has endocrine roles and participates in steroid metabolism. Insulin is produced by the pancreas, not fat cells. Central adiposity is associated with insulin resistance."], ["Routine Canadian folic acid", "Daily multivitamin containing 400 mcg (0.4 mg) folic acid for anyone who could become pregnant, ideally starting at least 3 months before. Higher-risk advice is individualized."], ["DFE versus folic acid", "1 mcg DFE equals 1 mcg food folate or 0.6 mcg folic acid consumed with food. DFE total intake and a folic-acid supplement recommendation are different quantities."], ["Dose versus total intake", "An RDA describes total intake, not a universal tablet dose. Example: pregnancy iron RDA 27 mg/day differs from Canadian pregnancy multivitamin advice 16–20 mg iron."], ["Females aged 19–50: nutrient reference values", "Nonpregnant → pregnant: iron 18→27 mg; B12: 2.4→2.6 mcg; iodine 150→220 mcg; choline AI 425→450 mg. These are total daily intakes, not prescriptions."], ["Calcium and vitamin D", "Calcium RDA 1,000 mg/day at 19–50, including pregnancy; ages 14–18 need 1,300 mg. Adult vitamin D RDA 15 mcg/600 IU total intake, distinct from practical supplement advice."], ["Reliable B12 and iodine", "Vegan eating requires a dependable B12 strategy. Check fortified-food labels and regular use. Iodine excess can also harm; kelp is not a casual treatment for thyroid concerns."], ["Miscarriage figures", "A percentage needs a denominator and time window. Test Q1’s 6–9% choices are not verified from the absent chapter. Chromosomal abnormalities account for many early losses; avoid blame."], ["Weight and fertility evidence", "Improved hormones or weight change do not guarantee conception/live birth. A BMI cutoff and a fixed athlete menarche delay are not universal diagnostic rules."], ["Maternal PKU", "Elevated maternal phenylalanine can harm fetal development, even if the fetus does not have PKU. Specialist preconception/pregnancy management is required."], ["Diabetes before pregnancy", "Arrange clinician-led glucose, medication and complication planning. Early hyperglycaemia is the developmental exposure; a prenatal vitamin does not replace this care."], ["Jane’s metabolic pattern", "Waist 36 in, triglycerides 175 mg/dL, HDL 35 mg/dL and fasting glucose 130 mg/dL fit four common metabolic-syndrome features; BP 110/70 mm Hg does not. Glucose also requires diabetes follow-up."], ["Critical test Q20 correction", "HDL 35 mg/dL is LOW. The printed option incorrectly calls HDL high. No option fully states the correct pattern; flag rather than memorize the error."], ["Fibre calculation ambiguity", "14 g/1,000 kcal × 2,000 kcal = 28 g/day, which is within 25–35 g/day. Test Q17 choices overlap; course context is needed."], ["Caffeine and fish limits in Canada", "Planning pregnancy: caffeine maximum 300 mg/day all sources. Listed high-mercury fish combined 150 g/month; canned albacore 300 g/week. These are different categories and time periods."]], "practice": [["Why start preparation before a positive pregnancy test?", "Early development can precede recognition; routines and clinical review take time. Waiting misses part of the prevention window, but starting after recognition is still worthwhile."], ["Is ovulation always on day 14?", "No. Cycles vary. A textbook example is not an individual prediction."], ["A client eats nutrient-dense foods but skips meals while training. What distinction matters?", "Food quality and enough energy are different. Investigate adequacy and symptoms without diagnosing from appearance."], ["Convert 0.4 mg folic acid to mcg.", "0.4 × 1,000 = 400 mcg. Keep the nutrient form unchanged during unit conversion."], ["Does 400 mcg DFE necessarily mean 400 mcg folic acid?", "No. DFE adjusts for source bioavailability; check the actual folic acid amount."], ["A supplement contains 300 mcg folic acid taken with food. What is its DFE contribution?", "300 ÷ 0.6 = 500 mcg DFE. This calculation does not show that it meets the Canadian 400 mcg folic-acid prevention recommendation."], ["Should everyone planning pregnancy take a 27 mg iron tablet?", "No. The 27 mg figure is a pregnancy total-intake RDA, not a universal preconception tablet prescription."], ["Does a normal-looking diet rule out B12 problems?", "No. Reliable supply, absorption and clinical context matter; appearance is not a nutrient assessment."], ["What does choline’s AI mean?", "It is an intake reference used when evidence is insufficient for an RDA, not a statement that choline is optional."], ["Two high-mercury fish portions are 80 g each in one month. Total and interpretation?", "160 g, above the Canadian 150 g/month combined limit for the specified group and fish. Do not substitute a weekly limit."], ["Caffeine amounts are 150, 80 and 30 mg. Can another 80 mg fit under 300 mg?", "Initial 260 mg; adding 80 gives 340 mg, above the maximum. Use actual product amounts in real life."], ["Why not double a multivitamin when more folic acid is being considered?", "All ingredients increase, not just folic acid. Higher-risk dosing needs individual professional assessment."], ["Does PCOS require the same low-carbohydrate diet for everyone?", "No. The guideline does not establish one superior dietary composition; individual needs and sustainability matter."], ["A fertility powder improves a laboratory marker. What key evidence is missing?", "Whether it improves meaningful clinical outcomes, such as pregnancy or live birth, with acceptable harms and in relevant populations."], ["A client with diabetes asks for a prenatal instead of a preconception appointment. Best response?", "Explain that supplements cannot replace glucose, medication and complication planning with the clinical team."], ["What makes a counselling goal useful?", "It is agreed, feasible, specific and reviewable. “Pack a workable lunch on three shifts this week” is more actionable than “eat perfectly for fertility.”"]]};
